+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS, and the server assigns each committed change an event revision. A self-hosted WebSocket service delivers committed changes to connected browsers with low latency. The change store retains replayable revisions and supports reliable publication so a delivery interruption cannot silently lose a committed change. Browsers reconnect after connection loss and catch up from their last applied revision before resuming live updates.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+This approach accepts responsibility for socket capacity, connection lifecycle, reconnect behavior, and catch-up operations in exchange for fast updates without coupling the board to a hosted realtime provider. HTTPS remains the command boundary; WebSockets deliver committed changes but do not establish authority or accept edits.
 
